N</p></div><div><p><span aria-hidden="true" style="background-color:#4d4d4d;width:16px;height:12px"></span>Mid-dark</p><p class="supporting">Lower-intermediate daytime IR brightness</p></div><div><p><span aria-hidden="true" style="background-color:#808080;width:16px;height:12px"></span>Mid</p><p class="supporting">Intermediate daytime IR brightness</p></div><div><p><span aria-hidden="true" style="background-color:#b3b3b3;width:16px;height:12px"></span>Mid-bright</p><p class="supporting">Higher-intermediate daytime IR brightness</p></div><div><p><span aria-hidden="true" style="background-color:#e6e6e6;width:16px;height:12px"></span>Bright</p><p class="supporting">High daytime infrared brightness; does not indicate heat hazard</p></div></div><p class="supporting">NASA/JPL · Arizona State University · THEMIS daytime IR controlled mosaic v2 · NASA Mars Trek · Relative daytime infrared brightness only (qualitative): the mosaic ships no verified radiometric scaling, so no temperature, thermal-inertia, rock-abundance or dust-cover values are asserted - the legend deliberately has no units. Daytime IR mixes albedo, surface slopes, and thermophysical properties, so bright/dark patches are not by themselves a compositional or hazard classification. Not a thermal-inertia product: the TES inertia map (3 km/px) is far too coarse for this region and is not used. Display-grade: tiles nearest-neighbour resampled 1:1 from source level L=z-1 by team/gale/prepare_thermal.mjs; the ~100 m source is upsampled above z9. Source alpha is preserved. The display shares the region display-transform caveats (planetocentric display latitude on Web Mercator).</p><p class="supporting">Coverage: THEMIS daytime infrared controlled mosaic v2 (Oct 2018, ~100 m/px) over the Gale Crater exploration area: derived display tiles cover 137.327-137.475 E, -4.839--4.564 N (Web-Mercator XYZ, zooms 5-9; above z9 the renderer upsamples the same source).</p><details class="product-metadata"><summary>Thermal (THEMIS daytime IR) data coverage and scale</summary><dl class="metadata"><div><dt>Original source product</dt><dd>THEMIS_DayIR_ControlledMosaics_100m_v2_oct2018 · Edwards et al. (2011), Christensen et al. (2004)</dd></div><div><dt>Source coverage</dt><dd>Global THEMIS controlled daytime infrared mosaic v2: near-global coverage between 60°S and 60°N on Mars 2000 Sphere.</dd></div><div><dt>Application display crop</dt><dd>Gale exploration area: 137.327–137.475° E, −4.839–−4.564° N (zooms 5–9). Mission View presentation bounds restrict display to the optical basemap footprint (~96.26 km²).</dd></div><div><dt>Valid-data coverage</dt><dd>100% valid over the Gale exploration area; no gaps within the observed scene. Relative brightness only.</dd></div><div><dt>Nominal posting</dt><dd>100 m; spacing of elevation samples, not measured effective resolution</dd></div><div><dt>North gradient support</dt><dd>Not applicable</dd></div><div><dt>Coordinate reference system</dt><dd>Mars 2000 sphere R=3,396,190 m, equirectangular planetocentric latitude and east longitude.</dd></div><div><dt>Scientific limitations</dt><dd>Relative daytime infrared brightness only (qualitative): the mosaic ships no verified radiometric scaling, so no temperature, thermal-inertia, rock-abundance or dust-cover values are asserted - the legend deliberately has no units. Daytime IR mixes albedo, surface slopes, and thermophysical properties, so bright/dark patches are not by themselves a compositional or hazard classification. Not a thermal-inertia product: the TES inertia map (3 km/px) is far too coarse for this region and is not used. Display-grade: tiles nearest-neighbour resampled 1:1 from source level L=z-1 by team/gale/prepare_thermal.mjs; the ~100 m source is upsampled above z9. Source alpha is preserved. The display shares the region display-transform caveats (planetocentric display latitude on Web Mercator).</dd></div></dl></details><details><summary>Source and processing details</summary><a href="https://trek.nasa.gov/tiles/Mars/EQ/THEMIS_DayIR_ControlledMosaics_100m_v2_oct2018/1.0.0/default/default028mm" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">NASA Trek - THEMIS Daytime Infrared Controlled Mosaic v2</a><p class="supporting">Global controlled daytime infrared mosaic v2 (released Oct 2018), Edwards et al. (2011), Christensen et al. (2004).</p><p class="supporting">Equirectangular planetary grid on Mars 2000 Sphere R=3,396,190 m, origin -180/90; nearest-neighbor 1:1 sampling from source matrix z-1 to Web-Mercator XYZ zooms 5-9.</p><p class="supporting">Represents relative daytime infrared brightness (qualitative DN). Uncalibrated for absolute kinetic temperature or thermal inertia in this composite mosaic; gaps outside data are transparent.</p><p class="supporting">Relative daytime infrared brightness only (qualitative); no physical temperature (K) or thermal inertia (J m^-2 K^-1 s^-1/2) values are asserted.</p><p class="supporting">Daytime IR mixes albedo, surface slopes, and thermophysical properties; bright or dark patches are not by themselves compositional, geotechnical, or astronaut hazard classifications.</p><p class="supporting">Not a live weather forecast or real-time thermal environment model. Coarse ~100 m resolution; localized suit hazards are completely unresolved.</p></details></div></section><section aria-label="Basemaps"><h3>Basemaps</h3><p class="supporting">Optical context and mission imagery.</p><p class="supporting">No dataset available.</p></section><section aria-label="Map overlays"><h3>Map overlays</h3><p class="supporting">Recorded traverses and dataset coverage outlines; these do not establish waypoint eligibility.</p><p class="supporting">No dataset available.</p></section><h3>Interface preview</h3><p class="supporting">Demonstration controls below affect schematic artwork only.</p><label class="check-row"><input type="checkbox"/>Show schematic preview <span class="badge science">Scenario</span></label><button class="full">View scientific data 